-- chem/twovertex.hypercubical — two vertices sharing one link, and the fact that the single-vertex magnetic loops
-- move no flux across it. A link joins end i of vertex u to end j of vertex v; the two ends are consistent when the
-- flux u sends into the link is the flux v receives, beyond(end i of u) = −beyond(end j of v). beyond is unchanged
-- by any arc loop at either vertex (chem/magnetic.hypercubical, loop-keeps-beyond; chem/magloop.hypercubical), so a
-- loop at u, or at v, keeps both sides' beyond and so keeps the match: the link carries exactly the flux it did.
-- The loops of chem/magloop act inside one vertex and conserve each vertex's charge (chem/transport.hypercubical),
-- and here they are shown to leave the shared link's flux fixed too. So no product of these arc loops moves flux
-- from u to v: the magnetic term, regularized on arcs at a vertex, carries no interaction between separated charges.
-- An inter-vertex interaction is therefore a loop that threads the link itself — a holonomy along the edge between
-- u and v, changing both ends' beyond together — not an arc at one vertex. That edge-threading loop is the next
-- object; near-zero thought has gone into it yet.
import "transport.hypercubical"

